Schur test: A majorizes B (same sum, A more spread) -- compare Gamma
====================================================================================
  4000 comparable pairs (same sum, one majorizes the other)
    Gamma larger on the MORE SPREAD set : 0
    Gamma larger on the MORE EQUAL set  : 3792
    equal                                : 208

  consistent with SCHUR-CONCAVE over this sample

====================================================================================
the weaker property that the closed form suggests: monotone in each element
====================================================================================
  raising one element by 2: 3627 trials, 0 decreases of Gamma
  (Gamma is nondecreasing in every element -- consistent with the closed form,
   whose weights are all positive.)

====================================================================================
and it is PROVABLE: the closed form is an ordered weighted average with
non-increasing weights, which is exactly the Schur-Ostrowski condition
====================================================================================
  layer form vs weighted form: 3000 sets, 0 disagreements
  weights for k = 7: [0.5, 0.25, 0.125, 0.0625, 0.03125, 0.01562, 0.01562]
  non-increasing: True

  Schur-Ostrowski: a symmetric phi is Schur-CONCAVE iff
      (a_i - a_j)(d phi/d a_i - d phi/d a_j) <= 0.
  Here d Gamma/d a_i = w_i with a sorted ASCENDING and w non-increasing, so
  a_i > a_j forces i > j forces w_i <= w_j, and the product is <= 0.  Done.
